{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790758163,"nonce":"B031ztymWjHShyFR9kyrEi9+i6I=","message":"A direct 20 ms clock gate at Ash's tier-one compile callback is unsafe with the current counter source. Beadie's declined-job retry horizon doubles the bead invocation count, and Ash stops ticking a callee after its caller compiles. On bench_method_call.hl with --preset benchmark --opt-threshold 1000 and ASH_TIER1_PROBE=1, the hot leaf (findex 23) reached tier-one proposal at 1,571 calls after 2.8 ms; its caller (findex 26) installed Cranelift at 6.5 ms. Ash then installed LLVM for the leaf at 11.6 ms. A 20 ms gate would reject the 2.8 ms proposal, and the caller could stop all future interpreter-visible ticks before the retry horizon. wren_lift's time gate assumes optimized-tier samples remain observable, which is not currently true for Ash direct calls. Next implementation should first provide native-tier heat samples or a transfer-aware promotion request, then use AIR ceiling and sustained heat to rank LLVM jobs. Keep benchmark preset's tier-exercising purpose explicit. No gate has been applied on this evidence.","files":null}]}